---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import corrections from "../data/corrections.json";
import additions from "../data/additions.json";
const allRows = corrections.groups.flatMap((g) => g.rows);
const lev = (k) => allRows.filter((r) => r.confidence === k).length;
const absent = additions.filter((a) => a.inTree === "\u2014");
const inTree = additions.filter((a) => a.inTree !== "\u2014");
---
<Base title="For Michael" eyebrow={corrections.count + " things the records say differently"}
      dek="Every place where a document and the family tree disagree, or where a document says something the tree does not say at all — with the source for each, so every line can be checked.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="grid g3">
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{corrections.count}</div><div class="l">corrections and additions</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">{corrections.documented}</div><div class="l">rest on a document that has been read</div></div>
      <div class="stat"><div class="n">0</div><div class="l">changed in the tree or the charts</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="prose">
      <p><strong>This page is addressed to the person who built the tree.</strong> An archive that
      only ever agrees with its source is not doing any work — so here is the list of places where
      this one disagrees, in the open, with the citation attached to every line.</p>
      <p><strong>Nothing here has been applied.</strong> The charts on this site draw Michael's
      export exactly as it was given, because it is his research and the corrections are proposals,
      not edits. Three of the twenty-seven are marked <em>candidate</em> or <em>flagged</em> rather
      than documented, which means the archive thinks it is probably right and is telling you it is
      not certain.</p>
      <p>The commonest single error is not a wrong name. <strong>It is a wrong year</strong> — and
      almost always a birth year, on a person the tree could not date from a document. Giovanni
      Arena is out by eleven years, Michele Donato by five, Salvatore Mazza by six. That pattern is
      worth knowing when you look at any other undated birth in the tree.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {corrections.groups.map((g) => (
    <section class="blk">
      <div class="lab">{g.kind}</div>
      <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:14px">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Who</th><th>The tree says</th><th>The record says</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            {g.rows.map((r) => (
              <tr>
                <td>{r.slug && r.slug !== "—"
                  ? <a href={u("/people/" + r.slug)}><strong>{r.who}</strong></a>
                  : <strong>{r.who}</strong>}
                  {r.confidence !== "documented" && <><br /><span class="small" style="color:var(--ink-3)">{r.confidence}</span></>}
                </td>
                <td class="small">{r.treeSays}</td>
                <td>{r.recordSays}</td>
                <td class="small">{r.source}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>
  ))}

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">People the records prove and the export does not contain</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">{absent.length} people who cannot appear on any chart here</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Every chart on this site is drawn from Michael's export. These people are not in it — so
      they cannot be drawn, however well documented they are.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Gen</th><th>Name</th><th class="m">Born</th><th>Trade</th><th>Parents</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {absent.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="m">{a.gen}</td>
              <td><strong>{a.name}</strong></td>
              <td class="m">{a.born}</td>
              <td>{a.trade !== "—" ? <em>{a.trade}</em> : ""}</td>
              <td class="small">{a.father !== "—" ? a.father + (a.mother !== "—" ? " × " + a.mother : "") : ""}</td>
              <td class="small">{a.source}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">And {inTree.length} this archive wrongly listed among them</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">In the tree after all</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>These {inTree.length} were published here for a time as people the family tree did not
      contain. That was wrong: the tree has all of them. What it does not have is their dates, their
      trades or their parents, which is what the registers supply — so the finding stands and only
      the framing was false. They are listed apart rather than quietly removed, because a correction
      this archive made to itself is worth as much as one it made to the tree.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Gen</th><th>Name</th><th>In the tree as</th><th class="m">Born</th><th>Parents</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {inTree.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="m">{a.gen}</td>
              <td><strong>{a.name}</strong></td>
              <td class="small"><a href={u("/people/" + a.inTree)}>{a.inTree}</a></td>
              <td class="m">{a.born}</td>
              <td class="small">{a.father !== "—" ? a.father + (a.mother !== "—" ? " × " + a.mother : "") : ""}</td>
              <td class="small">{a.source}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>How to read the confidence</h2>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:.6rem">Six labels, and the counts are counted rather than
    typed — two of these were in use here for days with no entry on this list, which is its own
    small lesson about legends written by hand.</p>
    <div class="rec" style="margin-top:16px">
      <div class="k">documented</div>
      <div class="v">A record has been read and it says this. You can open the citation and see it.</div>
      <div class="w">{lev("documented")}</div>
      <div class="k">certain</div>
      <div class="v">Established beyond doubt, but by setting <em>two</em> documents against each
      other rather than by reading one — an original against a chancery copy, a declared age against
      a birth act. Several of the entries above are corrections this archive made to a nineteenth-century
      copyist, and one is a correction it made to itself.</div>
      <div class="w">{lev("certain")}</div>
      <div class="k">probable</div>
      <div class="v">Either the best reading of an ambiguous document, or <strong>two documents that
      disagree</strong> — with the alternative stated in the row rather than hidden. Where a clerk
      crammed two ages into one clause, where a surname reached an index garbled, or where a register
      and a chancery copy give a woman two different names, this is what the answer is worth.</div>
      <div class="w">{lev("probable")}</div>
      <div class="k">inferred</div>
      <div class="v">No record names this person directly, but the step from what a record does say
      is a short one — someone travelling on the same day and the same ship, or a birthplace that
      only one of two candidate households can account for. <strong>An inference from circumstance,
      not from a hard-to-read hand.</strong></div><div class="w">{lev("inferred")}</div>
      <div class="k">candidate</div>
      <div class="v">The best match in an index, with something still not quite right about it —
      a spelling, an age. Probably correct. Not proved.</div><div class="w">{lev("candidate")}</div>
      <div class="k">flagged</div>
      <div class="v">A problem identified and deliberately <em>not</em> resolved, because resolving
      it would need a record nobody has read yet.</div><div class="w">{lev("flagged")}</div>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:22px">The full reasoning for each of these sits on the page it
    came from — <a href={u("/scilla/")}>Scilla</a> for the Calabrian registers,
    <a href={u("/crossings/")}>The Crossings</a> for the manifests,
    <a href={u("/nudgee/")}>Nudgee</a> for the burials and
    <a href={u("/queensland/")}>Queensland</a> for the BDM index. Where this archive has been
    <em>wrong</em>, that is on <a href={u("/method/")}>Method</a>, which is the more useful page.</p>
  </section>
</Base>
